   "h": "History, briefly — and where to hear it in people's own words",
   "text": "",
   "items": [
    "**Late 1800s:** a streetcar suburb for people who wanted to live outside downtown and still ride in to work. ✅ (*Northeast Narratives*.) Pendleton Heights was platted from the 200-acre Jones Farm. It is called the city's second-oldest planned residential neighborhood, after Quality Hill, and has the most true Victorian houses in Kansas City. ✅ (Wikipedia; KCUR 2022.)",
    "**Early 1900s:** lumber money and city planning. Robert A. Long built his mansion here in 1910; it is now the Kansas City Museum. George Kessler's park system put **Cliff Drive** and the 300-acre Kessler Park along the bluffs. ✅ (KCUR, Nov 2022.)",
    "**Immigrant waves:** Czech, Polish and Slovak families in the late 1800s; Italians in Little Italy (Columbus Park); Vietnamese refugees after the war; Bosnian refugees in the 1990s; later Somali and Sudanese families. ✅ (*Northeast Narratives*.) Mexican families came with the railroads: by the late 1920s about 90% of railroad laborers in the region were Mexican. ⚠️ (KCUR, Mar 2024; regional figure.)",
    "**Now:** restoration and rising prices next to vacant and failing houses — sometimes on the same block.",
    "**In their own words:** *Northeast Narratives* (Northeast News, 2024) — a resident-centered guide in English and Spanish that lets the neighborhood's organizers, teachers and immigrant communities tell its history. The Kansas City Public Library's **Missouri Valley Special Collections** holds local history. The North-East Branch Library runs **RISE** (Refugee & Immigrant Services & Empowerment) and has a welcome wall in about 15 languages. ✅ (*Northeast Narratives*.)"
   ]
