   "plain": "For most of the 1900s, where you could buy a house in Kansas City — and whether a bank would lend you the money to buy or fix one — was decided by maps, deed restrictions and lending rules. Troost Avenue became the line between white and Black Kansas City. Those decisions still show in who gets a loan today.",
