   "contested": "Whether investor buying saves vacant houses or pushes out the people who were there — both happen, sometimes on one block. Blight and revitalization are both words outsiders use for the same street. Who owns each empty house in the Northeast is knowable, parcel by parcel, but nobody has published it.",
   "who": "No single decision; many small ones. Cash buyers and investors gain discounted houses. The city gains tax revenue when a flipped house sells high, and pays for demolitions when one falls down. Neighbors who stay carry the risk: fire, lost value, or values that rise until they can't pay the taxes.",
   "nlb": "Nature: houses decay when empty. Luck: who owns the house beside yours. **Built:** what the land bank does with what it holds, how fast the city acts on dangerous buildings, whether a land trust exists, and the rules (or lack of them) on cash-buyer offers.",
   "doing": "Jerusalem Farm and partners renovated the St. Francis building so it would not be demolished. ✅ (Flatland 2022.) JF's land trust takes a house off the investor market for good. ✅ (Northeast News 2023.) Legal Aid of Western Missouri's **property donation** program lets an owner donate a house to a neighborhood group, which sells it to a contractor who must repair it. ✅ (Housing Court page.) Neighborhood associations track vacant houses. Jerusalem Farm's community research report goes to residents *and developers*, so newcomers hear what the neighborhood wants. ✅ (*Northeast Narratives*.)",
   "denver": "Denver-metro median rent is about $1,874/month, 31.9% of renter income; 49% of Colorado renters are cost-burdened.",
