 "cushionNotice": "Both cities are in the same country, under the same federal programs (Social Security, Medicare, SSDI, the 2025 Medicaid changes). Most of what differs is **state and city law**: the wage floor, paid sick leave (Colorado kept it; Missouri repealed it after four months), what a payday lender may charge (about 365% on average vs a 36% cap), how property is taxed and who gets a break. Stack the rows for one older Northeast homeowner and one Denver salaried family, and the same broken furnace is a nuisance in one house and a crisis in the other. RJ families are inside cushions they did not build: an employer's sick days, a credit card at a fraction of a payday rate, homeowners' insurance on a newer roof. That insulation is the fence we can't see (2b).",
 "fences": [
  {
   "h": "Insulation",
   "text": "Our houses are newer, our parents' jobs come with sick days, and if a roof fails, insurance and savings absorb it. So a peeling porch looks like a choice. See 2c."
  },
  {
   "h": "We fixed her house — the work as ours",
   "text": "The most specific fence on this trip. Students will do real, visible, satisfying work, and it is easy to tell it as the story: *we came, we fixed it.* But the homeowner has kept the house standing for decades, chose the repair, works beside the crew, and **repays the materials herself**. Our week of labor is one input to her project. Ask: whose work is this, and who will still be here next winter? The finished roof is not the point of the trip; the person under it is."
  },
  {
   "h": "Physical",
   "text": "Nobody from Denver goes to the Northeast on purpose. Kansas City, to us, is barbecue, the Chiefs, the Plaza, maybe the Royals — the side of the city visitors see. This trip is different: students sleep *in* the neighborhood, at Jerusalem Farm, and walk its streets. That is a gift. Notice where the van drives and where you walk, and which blocks you never see."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive",
   "text": "We think the U.S. is the place where effort works: *this is America; they could get a better job, sell, move.* We don't arrive knowing about lending maps, covenants, reassessment rules, or that a state can create sick leave and repeal it within a year. Without that, a boarded house looks like neglect instead of a stack of decisions. We also read old as bad; these are some of the most beautiful houses in the city."
  },
  {
   "h": "Affective",
   "text": "The pleasure of being helpful, which can need the neighbor to stay needy. Fear of the inner city, which RJ students may have absorbed without ever going there. Pity. And the opposite: romance — chickens, compost and simplicity can feel like a charming week of camping. For a family on $28,000 a year, simplicity is not a choice. Keep three things apart: what you saw, what you were told and by whom, and what you concluded."
  },
  {
   "h": "Theological",
   "text": "*Simplicity* is one of Jerusalem Farm's cornerstones. For us it is a chosen discipline for a week; for some neighbors it is imposed every week. Both can be holy, but they are not the same thing, and confusing them is a fence. Also: We're so blessed — reading our comfort as God's favor. And treating the Catholic Worker as charity only, when Dorothy Day also asked why the line at the soup kitchen existed."
  },
  {
   "h": "Destination snobbery",
   "text": "A domestic trip read as second-best: a short flight, regret at not going abroad, Kansas City ranked last among immersion choices two years running. The fence: believing that encounter needs a passport. The barriers in this packet are a two-hour flight away, and several have Denver twins."
  },
  {
   "h": "Comfort",
   "text": "Before the trip, the class asked whether there would be meat, daily showers, fast food, and whether they would lose fall break. The first day without phones was the hardest part for several. Name these honestly before the trip; they are the week's first lesson in what a cushion is."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cynicism about structure",
   "text": "The prayer bell felt at first like being controlled, then like a structure that held the day. Students who make that turn can be asked the same question about the city: which structures hold people, and which hold them down?"
  },
  {
   "h": "Only chores",
   "text": "Work that doesn't look like helping — fence posts, compost, sweet potatoes, a basement — felt like it didn't count until someone said whom it was for. The fence: needing service to be visible and to have a recipient in the frame."
  },
  {
   "h": "Group as refuge",
   "text": "Both 2024 papers end with classmates and chaperones as the real outcome. Friendship is a gift of the week; it can also be the way a group stays together and never meets the neighborhood."
  },
  {
   "h": "Fear of extremes",
   "text": "Students imagined sleeping outside or among bugs, and wanted to be back in a warm house while working outdoors in winter. Ask who in Kansas City, and in Denver, works outside all winter because the job requires it."
  },
  {
   "h": "Before the meal",
   "text": "Students describe arriving at the Catholic Worker house seeing unhoused people as statistics, or guessing at addiction, until a guest greeted them. That guess is the fence; the greeting is the encounter."
  }
 ],
